# Mutation plan for the user-update family (updateUser, patchUser, updateUserActive,
# updateUserRoles).
#
#   scripts/mutate-batch.sh scripts/mutations/user-update.plan
#
# The decisions worth mutating here are the ones whose wrong answer is a *successful* response:
#
#   * `tryingToChange`, whose inversion turns every profile-field lock into its opposite;
#   * the fill-in-once escapes on first and last name, which strand nameless accounts;
#   * the ORDER of the five locked-field returns, which is only observable to a patch that moves
#     two of them at once — the corpus grew four adjacent-pair rows for exactly this;
#   * the auto-responder's two transitions, which are NOT negations of one another;
#   * `NewSystemRoleIDs` membership as `any` rather than `all`;
#   * `MapFromJSON`'s partial decode, whose collapse-to-empty erases a user's roles;
#   * the trailing-bytes tolerance of both decoders, which is `Decoder.Decode` and not
#     `Unmarshal`;
#   * `user.Id != c.Params.UserId`, which is the only thing stopping a body from naming another
#     account;
#   * `patch.RemoteId = nil`, which is the only thing stopping a client claiming a remote row;
#   * which of the two routes propagates a credential error and which flattens it;
#   * `trustedUpdateData`, twice — the flag that decides whether a write may move `Roles` and
#     `DeleteAt` at all;
#   * which column `Sessions.Roles` is keyed on, an `Id`-for-`UserId` swap that updates one
#     device instead of all of them;
#   * and which half of `/active` is forwarded.
#
#
# # One survivor in the first pass, and it was a real finding about the code, not the tests
#
# `api-patch-keeps-the-remote-id` removed `patch.remote_id = None;` from `patchUser` and SURVIVED.
# The reason is that `SqlUserStore.Update` copies `RemoteId` off the stored row
# **unconditionally** — in the trusted path as well as the untrusted one — so the handler's
# nil-ing is defence in depth and nothing reachable through the REST API can tell the two apart.
# The right answer and the wrong answer coincide, which is the one situation where deleting the
# mutation is correct rather than a shrug.
#
# What replaced it asserts the protection that *is* load-bearing: the store's copy-back, which on
# `PUT /users/{id}` is the only thing there is — that route has no `SanitizeInput` and no explicit
# nil-ing. `the_body_cannot_grant_itself_roles_or_undelete_itself` grew two `remoteid` assertions
# in the same change, and without them this replacement would have survived too.
#
# Not mutated, and why: the last-administrator guard (`count <= 1`) has no test, because reaching
# a count of one on a shared stack means removing the fixture administrator. Recorded as a parity
# risk rather than asserted. `is_at_user_limit`'s `>=` is in the same position — the stack is
# nowhere near 250 users and nothing sends the input that separates `>` from `>=`.

